FerrionOS change to the Redox kernel (MIT license, kept), made against upstream commit e7f1d4231d0f4b55fbef229d9cd9459302c101f4 with ferrion-quiet-boot.patch, ferrion-boot-name.patch, ferrion-lockdown.patch, ferrion-scheme-short-read.patch and ferrion-event-call-unsubscribe.patch applied (Linux layer LC1, the foreign-call hook):
- a thread's handle `foreign-call` takes three words, an entry and the start and end of a native range; three zeros unregister. Only the thread itself may write it (EPERM otherwise), the range must lie in user memory and hold the entry (EINVAL), and other architectures answer EOPNOTSUPP;
- on x86_64 a `syscall` instruction outside the registered range is not dispatched: the thread resumes at the entry through a separate IRETQ exit with every general register as the instruction left it (RCX and R11 hold the return address and flags), so the call grants nothing the thread could not do by jumping there;
- the registration is copied into the percpu block on each switch, starts empty in every new context and is cleared when the context's address space changes (exec).

